egment  the narrative into thematic units and  this is important once we have like this  moment in time and there is a turning  point  the  plot will change it will progress into  another direction  and in this work we adopt a modern  scheme that has been put forward by  hague  in a book written in 2017 and i should  point out that these turning points are  the bread and butter of  screenwriters in particular in hollywood  and i forgot to say whatever i'm talking  about is how people do movies in  hollywood once i was giving another talk  and i was asked about bollywood and  i don't know uh chances are though that  most people have some turning points in  their movies so here's the uh modern  version you see on the screen of what  turning points look like and these are  the definitions that i will be using  throughout this talk so there is a setup  in the setup we have introduction of  characters and then something happens  there is an opportunity first turning  point  the opportunity because the turning  points progress the plot um  brings us into a new situation while  we're in the new situation there is a  change of plans plans have changed and  there is some progress in that direction  at which point we reach the point of no  return so both the hero the protagonist  and  co-protagonists are committed to a goal  there will be complications and a major  setback and while i'm talking this i  want you to  remember movies that you've seen and you  liked most movies follow this narrative  structure after the major setback there  will be the final push you either  overcome the setback or not there is a  climax and the movie ends  now i know this is a bit abstract so in  the next slide we're going to see  an example and as an example we will use  the shining the shining is a  psychological horror film  that was released in 1980 and was  produced and directed by stanley kubrick  and in fact it is based on a book  written by stephen king now when the  shining appeared in 